{"id":67721767285124,"type":"genre","name":"Urban Contemporary Gospel","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":116,"info":{"img":null},"own_scenes":[],"similar_scenes":[{"id":221413912922408,"name":"Frankie Crocker y WBLS","book":"The Come Up: An Oral History of the Rise of Hip-Hop","author":"Jonathan Abrams","start":1972,"end":1990,"desc":"El director de programación de WBLS-FM disparó la audiencia con el formato urban contemporary: disco, R&B, rock y luego hip hop sin etiquetas raciales, de Madonna a Hall & Oates. Era el maestro de ceremonias carismático cuyas rimas imitarían los MCs, y despedía cada noche con «Moody’s Mood for Love». Receloso del relevo, no negó la popularidad del hip hop: puso algunos de sus primeros discos y fichó a Mr. Magic. «No sé si el hip hop habría pasado en Nueva York sin Frankie Crocker» (Bill Stephney).","color":"#22d3ee","family":"Industria, medios y negocio","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/ComeUp/#frankie-crocker"},{"id":100588174081727,"name":"La industria del disco y la muerte del R&B","book":"Funk: The Music, the People, and the Rhythm of the One","author":"Rickey Vincent","start":1975,"end":1982,"desc":"Las emisoras negras se volvieron «urban contemporary» (WBLS en Nueva York, KSOL en San Francisco), con menos DJ negros y música menos negra; según Nelson George (The Death of Rhythm and Blues), Millie Jackson o Cameo pasaron a ser «demasiado negros»: un suicidio cultural. El «Harvard Report» de CBS trató el soul como producto; Neil Bogart (Casablanca: Donna Summer, Kiss, Village People, Parliament) inventó el bombo y las ventas imaginarias, y los independientes de promoción, la payola. Los artistas pagaban su promoción, había que llegar al oro para ganar algo y las tiendas pequeñas desaparecieron. Quien se oponía, como Jack «the Rapper» Gibson, era tachado de radical. El único consuelo: la América blanca también le declaró la guerra al disco. La Disco Demolition del estadio de los White Sox (12 de julio de 1979) acabó en disturbios; pero para el público rock «disco» pasó a significar toda música negra y el funk de finales de los setenta (P-Funk, Slave, Brick, Bar-Kays) quedó condenado con él.","color":"#e5e7eb","family":"Política y cultura negra","link":"https://volteret4.github.io/Funk/#industria-disco"}],"aka":[],"rym":[{"id":174684576091609,"name":"RYM Top — Scenes & Movements — Urban Contemporary Gospel","total":116,"url":"https://rateyourmusic.com/charts/top/album/all-time/g:urban-contemporary-gospel/","items":[{"id":147291218537688,"type":"album","name":"Acts of Faith","artist":"Sault","year":2024,"rank":1,"yt":"4Jg_nzG2Y1E"},{"id":230893472960321,"type":"album","name":"Jesus Is Born","artist":"Sunday Service Choir","year":2019,"rank":2,"yt":"F0k8UyEhC4k"},{"id":253384798118282,"type":"album","name":"Happy People / U Saved Me","artist":"R. Kelly","year":2004,"rank":3,"yt":"I_4dFHSFSso"},{"id":20594362576003,"type":"album","name":"The Nu Nation Project","artist":"Kirk Franklin","year":1998,"rank":4,"yt":"ihJDNQ6PdKE"},{"id":105606803677487,"type":"album","name":"Hiding Place","artist":"Tori Kelly","year":2018,"rank":5,"yt":"wGgsmCEgf4w"},{"id":85080636275295,"type":"album","name":"The Evolution of Gospel","artist":"Sounds of Blackness","year":1991,"rank":6,"yt":"6kxI1fcOjSY"},{"id":114820011857489,"type":"album","name":"Thankful","artist":"Mary Mary","year":2000,"rank":7,"yt":"_G84MxRDOw4"},{"id":130533510275351,"type":"album","name":"Camp Lukewarm","artist":"Lord's Child","year":2020,"rank":8,"yt":"pNZxp_m7Ers"},{"id":120198064909536,"type":"album","name":"Africa to America: The Journey of the Drum","artist":"Sounds of Blackness","year":1994,"rank":9,"yt":"pKj9t9VibS4"},{"id":68471872077141,"type":"album","name":"You Brought the Sunshine","artist":"The Clark Sisters","year":1981,"rank":10,"yt":"Pq-XKuzVgCM"},{"id":29554917728945,"type":"album","name":"Decisions","artist":"The Winans","year":1987,"rank":11,"yt":"-zZoKgiIj_E"},{"id":166893729745024,"type":"album","name":"Ye Shall Receive 